  <h1>DeepSeek Harness <span class="accent">vs Cursor</span></h1>
  <p class="muted small" style="margin-bottom:20px">Updated August 19, 2026 · originally August 14 · dshbase</p>

  <p>People search “DeepSeek Harness vs Cursor” as if one product should win. That was already the wrong frame in August 2026. Cursor is no longer “just Tab autocomplete.” It ships Agent, Plan, Ask, Debug, and Multitask. DeepSeek Harness (DSH) is no longer “a toy CLI.” It is an MIT-licensed agent runtime whose loop, sandbox, and surfaces are plugins.</p>
  <p>The useful question is: <strong>do you want an AI IDE, a runnable harness, or both?</strong></p>

  <h2>At a glance (August 2026)</h2>
  <div style="overflow-x:auto">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>DeepSeek Harness</th><th>Cursor</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Category</strong></td><td>Open-source agent harness</td><td>AI-native IDE (fork of VS Code)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>License</strong></td><td>MIT — you can fork the loop</td><td>Proprietary product</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Primary surface</strong></td><td>Web UI, headless CLI, TUI, Python SDK</td><td>Desktop IDE + Cloud Agents</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Autonomy (2026)</strong></td><td>Task in, plan + tools + files out; CI-friendly</td><td>Agent mode: plan, edit, terminal, browser MCP — you stay in the editor</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Extension</strong></td><td>Everything-is-a-plugin (<code>dsh plugin add</code>)</td><td>VS Code extensions + MCP + Cursor skills</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Models</strong></td><td>DeepSeek or any OpenAI-compatible endpoint</td><td>Cursor routing (and custom endpoints if you wire them)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Cost shape</strong></td><td>Free harness + your API bill</td><td>Subscription + included / extra usage</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Unattended / CI</strong></td><td>First-class (<code>dsh --profile headless</code>)</td><td>Possible via Cloud Agents / scripts; not the default mental model</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Editor UX</strong></td><td>Thin (file browser web UI, no full IDE)</td><td>Diffs, debugger, tabs, search, Git — the reason you open it</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What changed since the first draft</h2>
  <p>Our original August 14 note said Cursor “doesn’t run long autonomous tasks.” That is outdated. Cursor Agent can inspect a repo, edit many files, run commands, and drive a logged-in browser. If you only remember the 2025 Cursor, update the picture.</p>
  <p>What did <em>not</em> change: Cursor is still an IDE product. DSH is still a harness you can install, pin, and run without that IDE. Overlap grew; the category split remains.</p>

  <h2>Where they overlap</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Multi-step coding work: read, patch, test, iterate.</li>
    <li>Tool use: shell, files, search, MCP-style connectors.</li>
    <li>You still need to review diffs. Neither is a substitute for ownership of the repo.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where DeepSeek Harness still wins</h2>
  <p><strong>You own the runtime.</strong> On dsh 0.1.0-rc.6 we verified that model adapter, session storage, sandbox, and the agent loop itself are swappable plugins. That is a different contract from “use our app.”</p>
  <p><strong>Headless is the product, not a side door.</strong></p>
  <pre class="code-block"><code>dsh --profile headless "inspect the repo and fix the failing tests"</code></pre>
  <p>Cron, GitHub Actions, a Linux box in Los Angeles — same command. Cursor can automate, but the center of gravity is the editor window.</p>
  <p><strong>Plugin density for the harness itself.</strong> Our catalog currently lists <strong>1,780</strong> DSH plugins (npm + git sources), with daily install tests on a subset. Cursor’s marketplace is huge for <em>editors</em>; DSH’s npm graph is huge for <em>agent capabilities</em> (IM bridges, skins, memory, vision, catalog search). Browse the <a href={directoryUrl}>directory</a>.</p>
  <p><strong>Cost routing.</strong> Point DSH at DeepSeek (or any compatible API) and you pay the model, not an IDE seat, for overnight batch work. For a longer cost discussion see our <a href={getRelativeLocaleUrl('en', '/blog/deepseek-harness-cost/')}>cost &amp; caching notes</a>.</p>

  <h2>Where Cursor still wins</h2>
  <p><strong>The editor is the workbench.</strong> Inline Tab, precise diffs, debug, multi-root, language servers — DSH’s web UI is not trying to replace that. Community shells exist; see <a href={desktopUrl}>desktop alternatives</a>.</p>
  <p><strong>Modes in one place.</strong> Plan / Ask / Agent / Debug / Multitask is a productized workflow. DSH has Standard / PTC / Minimal / Create, but they live in a harness, not in a decade of IDE muscle memory.</p>
  <p><strong>Team and review culture.</strong> Many shops already live in VS Code keybindings. Cursor is the lowest-friction way to put an agent <em>inside</em> that culture.</p>

  <h2>A practical split (what we actually recommend)</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Interactive changes, UI polish, reading a diff:</strong> Cursor (or any IDE).</li>
    <li><strong>Repeatable jobs, plugin verification, “leave it running on a server”:</strong> DSH headless / web profile.</li>
    <li><strong>Same human, same week:</strong> draft in Cursor, hand a bounded task to DSH, or the reverse. They are complementary, not a religious choice.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>If you are choosing against Claude Code rather than against Cursor, read <a href={vsCcUrl}>DSH vs Claude Code</a> — that comparison is closer (two agent harnesses).</p>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <h3>Can DeepSeek Harness replace Cursor?</h3>
  <p>For “open a file, jump to definition, debug a test in the IDE” — no. For “run this task on a box with no GUI” — DSH is usually the better fit.</p>
  <h3>Can Cursor replace DeepSeek Harness?</h3>
  <p>For daily in-editor agent work, often yes. For self-hosted loops, CI agents, and “I need to swap the harness internals,” no.</p>
  <h3>Does DSH need a GPU?</h3>
  <p>No. It is Node.js talking to an API. Install starts at <code>npx @deepseek-ai/dsh web</code> plus Node LTS. Details: <a href={installUrl}>install guide</a>.</p>
  <h3>Can I point DSH at Cursor’s models?</h3>
  <p>Only if those models are exposed as an OpenAI-compatible endpoint. DSH does not speak Cursor’s private router.</p>
